.orm.users.select('id', 'email').all()` — not `db.orm.public.User`. Full rule and rewrite recipe in `references/queries.md` § *MongoDB ORM addressing*.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md:89:const user = await db.orm.users.create({\nskills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:78:const created = await db.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com', name: 'Alice' });\nskills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:166:The rule to remember: inside a request, `db` does everything the `db` from `postgres()` does, so any documented `db.orm...`, `db.sql...`, `db.raw...`, `db.transaction(...)`, `db.prepare(...)` or `db.query(...)` snippet works unchanged. Await every query before the `await using` scope ends. The connection's `close()` waits until the runtime has been idle for one turn of the event loop (a `setTimeout(0)`), then refuses new work. A lazy ORM read such as `db.orm.public.User.all()`, or `db.query(plan)`, returned from the scope without `await` starts only when the caller awaits it, after the scope has closed, so it rejects with `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` (\"Runtime is closed\"), whose `fix` names the missing `await`. Work that keeps the runtime busy from the close onward completes: a `first()`, `execute()` or `transaction()`, an ORM write and its reload, including `include(...).create()` and a nested `create()`, and an async helper whose steps go through `db.orm`, `db.transaction()` or `db.prepare()`; a transaction commits. Such work is never refused because of the close; if it fails for its own reason, it rejects like any unawaited promise. A helper that calls `db.runtime()` after the scope has ended throws \"Postgres connection is closed\" at that call. Work that starts after the runtime was idle for a turn is refused. Whether a query sent after waiting on something other than the database counts as idle depends on when that callback runs relative to the timer, so do not rely on it. An unawaited promise that fails then, or fails for its own reason, rejects like any unawaited promise, and Node reports it as unhandled. Write `return await db.orm...`, not `return db.orm...`.\nskills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:364:  const user = await tx.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com' });\nskills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md:365:  await tx.orm.public.Post.create({ userId: user.id, title: 'hello' });\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:108:      `MongoRuntimeOptions` is `{ context, adapter, driver, middleware?, mode? }`, as the SQL runtime's options are: the caller creates the adapter with `stack.adapter.create(stack)`, and the runtime checks middleware against `context.contract.target`, so `context` is a `MongoExecutionContext<MongoContract>`. `MongoExecutionContext` is declared in `@prisma/orm-family-mongo/query-ast/execution` (`@internal/mongo-query-ast/execution`), still exported from the runtime entry; it has no `stack` member and no second type parameter (`TTargetId`), and its type parameter is constrained as SQL's `ExecutionContext` is: `MongoExecutionContext<TContract extends MongoContract = MongoContract>`, and `createMongoExecutionContext` takes the same constraint. `createMongoExecutionStack()` still takes an optional `driver`; given one, it returns `MongoExecutionStackWithDriver`, whose `driver` is always set, and without one it returns `MongoExecutionStack`, which has no `driver` member, as `createSqlExecutionStack()` does. `buildMongoEnums()` and `MongoEnumAccessors` are removed, and `MongoExecutionContext` has no `enums` member, as SQL's `ExecutionContext` has none: the static context builds `db.enums` with the framework's `buildNamespacedEnums`, as `postgresStatic()` does, and the ORM builds the accessors it checks writes against from the context, once per client. The detection matches files that call `createMongoRuntime(`, read `.stack` from a context, give `MongoExecutionContext` two type arguments or a non-contract type argument, or name `buildMongoEnums` or `MongoEnumAccessors`.\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:251:const driverInstance = clientStack.driver.create();\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:291:const driver = stack.driver.create();\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:295:Replace `MongoDriverImpl.fromDb(client.db(dbName))` with `driver.connect({ kind: 'mongoClient', client, dbName })` on a driver created the same way. Code that only sends DDL through a control adapter can use the control driver instead: `await mongoControlDriver.create(url)`, with `mongoControlDriver` the default export of `@prisma/orm-target-mongo/driver/control`. The control driver uses the database the URL names, so put `dbName` in the URL's path, for example `mongodb://localhost:27017/app`.\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.21-to-8.0.0-rc.22/instructions.md:381:const runtime = createMongoRuntime({ context, adapter: stack.adapter.create(stack), driver });\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.11-to-0.12/strip-migration-labels-hints.ts:90:  const sorted: Record<string, unknown> = Object.create(null);\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.14-to-8.0.0-rc.15/instructions.md:1642:const ast = sqlFamilyDescriptor.create(controlStack).inferPslContract(rawSchemaNode);\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.16-to-0.17/strip-sha256-hash-prefixes.ts:106:  const sorted: Record<string, unknown> = Object.create(null);\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.11-to-8.0.0-rc.12/instructions.md:728:const familyInstance = family.create(stack);\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.20-to-8.0.0-rc.21/instructions.md:484:    return postgresDriverDescriptor.create({ cursor }, sessionSettings);\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.22-to-8.0.0-rc.23/instructions.md:93:      A MongoDB migration creates and drops each index by its name. `prisma migration plan` writes the name into `migration.ts`, as in `createIndex(\"users\", [{ field: \"email\", direction: 1 }], { name: \"User_email_key\", unique: true })` and `dropIndex(\"users\", [{ field: \"email\", direction: 1 }], { name: \"User_email_key\", unique: true })`. The operation id is `index.<collection>.<create|drop>(<name>)`, such as `index.users.create(email_1)`, where it was `index.users.create(email:1)`. A `migration.ts` written by an earlier release keeps working: an index without a name is named after its keys, as before.\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.22-to-8.0.0-rc.23/instructions.md:414:A script or test that matches an operation id, such as `index.users.create(email:1)`, matches the name instead: `index.users.create(email_1)`.\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:263:      On a client that closes at once (`postgres({ contract, url })`, `sqlite()`, `supabase()`), an ORM call that resolves to one value, such as `first()`, `create()`, `include(...).create()` or a nested create, starts when it is called, and `close()` waits for it, so one left pending at `close()` now completes instead of rejecting: a `create()` used to reject with \"Runtime is closed\", and `include(...).create()` and a nested create with \"Postgres client is closed\". A streaming read such as `all()` starts when it is read; read after `close()`, it rejects with \"Postgres client is closed\" instead of \"Runtime is closed\". Update code or tests that expect the old outcome.\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1026:await db.orm.public.Post.create({ title: 'Hello', publishedAt: new Date() as unknown as string });\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.18-to-8.0.0-rc.19/instructions.md:1029:await db.orm.public.Post.create({ title: 'Hello', publishedAt: new Date().toISOString() });","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015UHQE7nQWZ4Dk6xCYHCjeB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
